## TODO: pak-entries-schema

Phase 2, only after pak-analyzer is verified. Work happens in modrex-index.

- New table in index.db:
  pak_entries(sha256 REFERENCES file_contents, entry_path, entry_hash, entry_size)
  plus a per-sha256 manifest status (ok / encrypted / no-directory-index), keyed on
  CONTENT hash so identical paks shipped by multiple mods store one manifest.
- Safety constraint that drove the design (verified against modrex-main's reader): all
  five queries in src-tauri/src/commands/mod_index.rs name their columns explicitly and
  open read-only, so NEW tables and columns are invisible to shipped apps - safe. What
  is NOT safe is adding rows to the mods table for mods without indexed files:
  query_by_name identifies a mod only when exactly one LIKE match exists, so bloating
  mods would silently break name identification. pak_entries must not touch existing
  tables.
- Wire the parser into extractContentEntries in build-index.ts: the pak bytes are
  already in memory there when the SHA256 is computed, so new mods get manifests with
  zero extra downloads or API calls.
- Backfill for the ~7,500 already-indexed PD3/Crime Boss pak files (they need one
  re-download to parse their indexes): mirror the existing entry_name refill mechanism,
  i.e. a getFileIdsMissingManifests set (like getFileIdsMissingNames) that --backfill
  honors and that checkIsCurrent treats like its missing-set parameter, so builder-state
  check skips do not suppress the refill. Do NOT use --recheck-all for this: that would
  re-examine all ~29k mods including PD2/PDTH, whose marker files are not paks and are
  excluded from manifests entirely.

